<!--
Bundle of 383 files from the directory tree 'asciidoctor-pdf', created by bundle.py
(skill bundle-files). It archives a project in one text file for AI agents.

This is NOT well-formed XML. File contents are embedded verbatim without escaping,
so an XML parser will fail. Read it as plain text.

How to read it without loading everything:
1. Read the table of contents from the top of the file up to the line '</toc>'.
   It lists all directories depth-first; level is the depth below the top, and an
   abstract summarizes the directory's whole subtree (level 0: the whole project).
   In a large TOC read the dir and abstract lines of low levels first, then only
   the entries of the subtrees you need.
2. Each entry gives start=S lines=N: the file's content is on lines S to S+N-1
   of this file. Read only that range (e.g. a line-based read with offset S, limit N).
Entries with converted='pdf' (or docx, ...) hold Markdown extracted from the original
document; entries with binary='true' have no content.
-->
<documents>
<toc abstract-level="3">
<dir path="asciidoctor-pdf" level="0">
<abstract>
Source snapshot of Asciidoctor PDF (gem version 3.0.0.alpha.0, main branch at commit d32ddf03, 2026-10-15): the native Ruby converter that turns AsciiDoc into PDF without an intermediate format. It runs on Asciidoctor 2.x and renders with Prawn 2.4 plus prawn-table, prawn-svg 0.40, prawn-icon 4.1 (FontAwesome 6), prawn-templates (imported PDF pages) and treetop (inline formatting parser). Use this bundle as the authoritative reference when the documentation is silent or ambiguous: how a block, attribute, role, theme key or CLI option is actually handled, which edge cases are supported, and which messages get logged.

Where to look: lib/asciidoctor/pdf/converter.rb is the single large converter class; it has one convert_<node> method per AsciiDoc node (convert_section, convert_admonition, convert_table, convert_image, convert_inline_quoted, ...) and ink_* methods for page furniture (title page, cover, TOC, running content, footnotes, chapter titles). theme_loader.rb resolves and evaluates theme YAML (extends, variables, math, colors, units); data/themes holds the built-in themes (base, default, default-sans, *-for-print, *-with-font-fallbacks) whose keys show every default value. formatted_text/ contains the inline markup parser and transforms that map HTML-like inline output to Prawn fragments. ext/ patches Prawn and its plugins (page/column handling, font fallback, table cells, SVG loading, Rouge/CodeRay highlighting).

The spec/ directory is the best source for edge cases: each *_spec.rb file covers one feature, and every `it` block contains an AsciiDoc input plus the expected PDF result (text positions, fonts, colors, page numbers, log messages). Search it with --search for an attribute, role or theme key to find the exact behavior. spec/fixtures holds the AsciiDoc inputs, custom themes and images those specs load. docs/ is the Antora documentation (user guide in ROOT, theming guide in theme, extension guide in extend); examples/ contains sample documents and themes, including an edge-cases document.

Build and test (only needed to verify a hypothesis): bundle install, then bundle exec rspec (tags ~network and ~visual skip network and visual-comparison tests). Convert a document with asciidoctor-pdf doc.adoc or asciidoctor -r asciidoctor-pdf -b pdf doc.adoc; select a theme with -a pdf-theme=name-or-path and -a pdf-themesdir, fonts with -a pdf-fontsdir. Not included in the bundle: the reference PDFs used for visual tests, the bundled font files, CI and release tasks.
</abstract>
